            "id": "4951679",
            "title": "Fat 11% Dividend Yield From MFA Financial's Preferred Share",
            "description": "<html><body><figure class=\"getty-figure\" data-type=\"getty-image\"><picture> <img alt=\"A young chocolate labrador puppy lies in the sun on the back of the sofa - sweet dreams!\" data-id=\"2277296961\" data-type=\"getty-image\" fetchpriority=\"high\" height=\"1022\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 132px), (max-width: 1199px) calc(100vw - 666px), (max-width: 1307px) calc(100vw - 708px), 600px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2277296961/image_2277296961.jpg?io=getty-c-w630\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2277296961/image_2277296961.jpg?io=getty-c-w1536 1536w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2277296961/image_2277296961.jpg?io=getty-c-w1280 1280w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2277296961/image_2277296961.jpg?io=getty-c-w1080 1080w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2277296961/image_2277296961.jpg?io=getty-c-w750 750w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2277296961/image_2277296961.jpg?io=getty-c-w640 640w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2277296961/image_2277296961.jpg?io=getty-c-w480 480w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2277296961/image_2277296961.jpg?io=getty-c-w320 320w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/2277296961/image_2277296961.jpg?io=getty-c-w240 240w\" width=\"1536\"/> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"> </p> <p class=\"item-credits\">Justin Paget/DigitalVision via Getty Images</p></figcaption></figure><p data-eci=\"true\">MFA Financial (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/MFA\" title=\"MFA Financial, Inc.\">MFA</a>) has a preferred share we should’ve told readers about more often: MFA-C (<span class=\"ticker-hover-wrapper\"><a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/MFA.PR.C\" title=\"MFA Financial, Inc. 6.50 PFD SER C\">MFA.PR.C</a></span>).</p> <p>Apparently, we've been neglecting it.</p> <p>That's unfortunate because MFA-C currently offers investors a massive stripped yield around 11.00%. And the good news is that it’s in our buy range. That's a refreshing change after <a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/article/4951557-10_24-percent-dividend-yield-from-franklin-bsp-realty-preferred-share-remains-boring\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://seekingalpha.com/article/4951557-10_24-percent-dividend-yield-from-franklin-bsp-realty-preferred-share-remains-boring\">our article on FBRT-E</a> (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/FBRT.PR.E\" title=\"Franklin BSP Realty Trust, Inc. PFD SER E\">FBRT.PR.E</a>) that came out recently.</p> <p>MFA-C is kind enough to offer investors a lower risk rating along with a significantly higher dividend yield.</p> <h2>MFA-C</h2> <p>MFA-C recently traded around $22.49:</p> <figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"153\" data-og-image-facebook=\"false\" data-og-image-google_news=\"false\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"false\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"false\" data-og-image-msn=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"false\" data-width=\"1341\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-17909594120549464_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"chart\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"73\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-17909594120549464_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-17909594120549464_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-17909594120549464_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-17909594120549464_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-17909594120549464_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"><span>The REIT Forum</span></p></figcaption></figure><p>At that price, shares are trading at about 93.7% of our buy target.</p> <p>Our current price targets are:</p> <ul> <li><p>Strong Buy Under: $22.07</p></li> <li><p>Buy Under: $23.99</p></li> <li><p>Overpriced: $25.44</p></li> </ul> <p>That puts MFA-C well into our buy range and decently close to our strong buy range. Shares come with a risk rating of 3.5 on our scale from 1 to 5. That's certainly not our lowest risk rating, but investors are being compensated with a stripped yield around 11%.</p> <p>If you’re an income investor, or just an investor, MFA-C is a share worth paying attention to.</p> <h2>Floating Rate</h2> <figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"152\" data-og-image-facebook=\"false\" data-og-image-google_news=\"false\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"false\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"false\" data-og-image-msn=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"false\" data-width=\"1489\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-1790959408650601_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"chart\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"65\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-1790959408650601_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-1790959408650601_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-1790959408650601_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-1790959408650601_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-1790959408650601_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"><span>The REIT Forum</span></p></figcaption></figure><p>MFA-C <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1055160/000119312520050212/d829118d424b5.htm\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1055160/000119312520050212/d829118d424b5.htm\">originally carried a fixed coupon rate of 6.50%</a>, but shares changed to floating on March 31, 2025. The floating rate was originally going to be three-month LIBOR plus a 5.345% spread. However, as many readers will know, LIBOR is no longer with us.</p> <p>Instead, <a href=\"https://www.mfafinancial.com/sec-filings/all-sec-filings/content/0001055160-26-000004/mfa-20251231.htm\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://www.mfafinancial.com/sec-filings/all-sec-filings/content/0001055160-26-000004/mfa-20251231.htm\">MFA went to three-month CME Term SOFR plus a 0.26161% adjustment and the original 5.345% spread</a>. This is from that SEC filing:</p> <figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"441\" data-og-image-facebook=\"false\" data-og-image-google_news=\"false\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"false\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"false\" data-og-image-msn=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"false\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"false\" data-width=\"1884\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-17909594088584745_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"image\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"150\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-17909594088584745_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-17909594088584745_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-17909594088584745_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-17909594088584745_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/2/14527192-17909594088584745_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"><span>SEC</span></p></figcaption></figure><p>This is a floating-rate share. If short-term interest rates decline, the dividend rate would decline as well. On the flip side, if short-term rates increase, the dividend rate would also increase. We don’t need to predict where interest rates will be in a year (no one can) to know the stripped yield is around 11% today.</p> <p><em>Somehow</em>, MFA managed to handle the end of LIBOR without deciding shareholders should get a dramatically smaller dividend.</p> <h2>Relative Value Matters</h2> <p>Recently, we wrote about FBRT-E.</p> <p>FBRT-E was trading around $18.79 with a stripped yield around 10.24% as of writing that article. That’s not a bad yield, but remember it comes with a risk rating of 4. MFA-C carries a risk rating of 3.5 and offers a stripped yield around 11.00%.</p> <p>Investors can take on more risk for less yield with FBRT-E. That's an option.</p> <p>We wouldn't call it our favorite option.</p> <p>This is precisely why we spend so many hours comparing preferred shares on a relative basis. If you’re looking at a share in isolation, you could easily come to the conclusion that a 10% yield looks fantastic. Then you’d miss the share just in the next room that has a lower risk rating <strong>and</strong> offers an 11% yield.</p> <p>Awkward.</p> <p>For investors interested in shares with a lower risk rating, <a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/article/4950724-9-6-percent-dividend-yield-from-dynex-capital-preferred-share-flirts-with-a-buy\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://seekingalpha.com/article/4950724-9-6-percent-dividend-yield-from-dynex-capital-preferred-share-flirts-with-a-buy\">we also recently covered DX-C</a> (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/DX.PR.C\" title=\"Dynex Capital, Inc. 6.9% SER C PFD\">DX.PR.C</a>) from Dynex Capital (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/DX\" title=\"Dynex Capital, Inc.\">DX</a>). DX-C carries a risk rating of 1.5. That's significantly lower than MFA-C's 3.5. As of writing that article, its stripped yield was around 9.62% and shares were cents out of our buy range.</p> <p>Investors have a few choices here. DX-C offers less risk with a lower yield. MFA-C offers significantly more income in exchange for more risk. FBRT-E offers more risk than MFA-C while offering less income.</p> <p>We appreciate FBRT-E volunteering to make this comparison easier.</p> <h2>Call Risk</h2> <p>MFA-C could be called. MFA can call shares for $25.00 with a <a href=\"https://www.mfafinancial.com/sec-filings/all-sec-filings/content/0001193125-20-056234/0001193125-20-056234.pdf\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://www.mfafinancial.com/sec-filings/all-sec-filings/content/0001193125-20-056234/0001193125-20-056234.pdf\">30 to 60 day notice</a>. However, $22.49 is materially below $25. Consequently, we believe a call here would be an odd way for MFA to try and save money. If MFA wants to reduce the shares outstanding, it could purchase them at the market price.</p> <p>It doesn't look like MFA is particularly interested in reducing shares. You can see on <a href=\"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1055160/000105516026000015/mfa-20260630.htm\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1055160/000105516026000015/mfa-20260630.htm\">page 41 of this 10-Q</a> that MFA issued over 100,000 more of its MFA-C shares earlier this year.</p> <h2>Investing In Preferred Shares and Baby Bonds</h2> <p><span class=\"highlighted_text\">We spend a great deal of time comparing preferred shares and baby bonds at<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/mp/1060-the-reit-forum/articles\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://seekingalpha.com/mp/1060-the-reit-forum/articles\"> The REIT Forum</a>. We recently wrote a<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/instablog/14527192-colorado-wealth-management-fund/6294205-guide-to-swapping-preferred-shares\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://seekingalpha.com/instablog/14527192-colorado-wealth-management-fund/6294205-guide-to-swapping-preferred-shares\"> guide on swapping preferred shares</a>. The basic concept is simple. When two similar preferred shares get too far apart on valuation, investors can sell the more expensive share and move into the cheaper one.</span></p> <p><span class=\"highlighted_text\">For readers who want to see how often we actually do this, we also published our<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/instablog/14527192-colorado-wealth-management-fund/6298912-listing-my-100-latest-trades-in-preferred-shares-and-baby-bonds\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://seekingalpha.com/instablog/14527192-colorado-wealth-management-fund/6298912-listing-my-100-latest-trades-in-preferred-shares-and-baby-bonds\"> most recent 100 trades in preferred shares and baby bonds</a>. We find these opportunities fairly often in the preferred shares of mortgage REITs.</span></p> <h2>Final Thoughts</h2> <p>MFA-C isn't a low-risk preferred share. For conservative investors, this may not be a great option compared to something like DX-C which comes with a risk rating of 1.5. However, investors willing to take on that risk are getting paid for it.</p> <p>At $22.49, MFA-C is trading at roughly 93.7% of our buy target. It also comes with a stripped yield around 11.00%. Trading under call value and having a yield that massive is attractive enough for us to own shares.</p> <p>There are safer preferred shares available, but sadly none of them are this far into our buy range. There are also preferred shares that are offering materially more risk while handing investors less income.</p> <p>We just wrote about one of them.</p> <p>Thanks, FBRT-E.</p> <p><em>Note: As of writing this article, we had DX preferred shares as a risk rating of 1.5. We are reviewing those preferred shares and there is a material chance we upgrade them to a risk rating of 1.</em></p> </body></html>",
